Title: [proposal] - Add `EdgeInsetsOverlay` to the widgets library

Description:
## 1. Overview

This proposal introduces a new layout widget, `EdgeInsetsOverlay`, to the Flutter `widgets` library.

`EdgeInsetsOverlay` positions edge-docked overlays (`top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right`) over a full-bleed content area and provides the measured overlay dimensions as an `EdgeInsets` parameter directly to an `EdgeInsetsOverlayWidgetBuilder` callback.

This enables background visuals (maps, video viewports, feeds, custom painters) to extend edge-to-edge behind translucent or floating controls, while giving child content full, explicit awareness of edge obstructions—without mutating or corrupting `MediaQuery`.

## 2. Background & Motivation

### 2.1 The Problem

Modern UI designs frequently require full-bleed background content (such as interactive maps, media feeds, or camera viewports) with floating or translucent controls docked along the screen edges (such as search bars, floating action buttons, navigation bars, or checkout sheets).

Currently, achieving this pattern in Flutter poses several challenges:

1. **`Column` / `Row` vs. `Stack` Dilemma:**
   - Using `Column` or `Row` partitions layout space, preventing child content from rendering edge-to-edge behind translucent overlays.
   - Using `Stack` allows full-bleed background rendering, but children inside the stack have no built-in awareness of the overlays' sizes.

2. **Manual Measurement Overhead:**
   - Developers must manually measure overlay heights/widths using `GlobalKey`, `LayoutBuilder`, or custom callbacks and manually pass these padding values down the widget tree.
   - This approach is error-prone, introduces unnecessary rebuild frames, and breaks when overlay sizes change dynamically (e.g., text scaling, screen orientation, keyboard visibility).

3. **Safe Area & Edge Inset Coordination:**
   - Edge overlays typically need to absorb system intrusions (such as the notch or navigation bar) at their outer boundary, while the main background underneath needs to coordinate its own visual center or scroll padding with the overlay dimensions.

### 2.2 Initial Exploration: `SafeAreaOverlay` (The `MediaQuery` Mutation Approach)

An initial concept explored was `SafeAreaOverlay`, which measured edge overlays and automatically injected their dimensions into the descendant `MediaQueryData.padding`.

This architecture was directly inspired by how Flutter's Material `Scaffold` handles `extendBody` and `extendBodyBehindAppBar`:
- In `Scaffold`, `_ScaffoldLayout` measures edge-docked bars (`appBar`, `bottomNavigationBar`, `persistentFooterButtons`) and passes their dimensions to `_BodyBuilder` via `_BodyBoxConstraints`.
- `_BodyBuilder` wraps the `body` in a `MediaQuery` whose `padding` accounts for the bar heights, allowing descendant `SafeArea`s or scroll views to avoid obstructions automatically.

The intention was to broaden the concept of "Safe Area" beyond strictly operating system intrusions (status bars, notches) to encompass any application-level edge obstruction.

### 2.3 Architectural Controversies & Pitfalls of Mutating `MediaQuery`

However, mutating `MediaQuery` padding to represent application-level bars is historically one of the most controversial and bug-prone layout mechanisms in Flutter:

1. **Semantic Distortion of `MediaQuery`:** `MediaQueryData` is architecturally intended to reflect physical window and OS-level properties (display cutouts, status bars, system navigation bars, software keyboards). Injecting local widget dimensions into `MediaQuery.padding` blurs the line between hardware constraints and application-level layout.
2. **Double-Padding Hazards (`CustomScrollView`, `SliverAppBar`, `SafeArea`):** When `MediaQuery.padding` is augmented with bar dimensions, descendant widgets (such as `SafeArea`, `SliverSafeArea`, or pinned sliver headers) frequently apply double the intended padding unless developers manually insert `MediaQuery.removePadding`.
3. **Keyboard & `viewInsets` Desynchronization:** `MediaQueryData` establishes strict relationships between `padding`, `viewPadding`, and `viewInsets`. When a software keyboard opens, `SafeArea` with `maintainBottomViewPadding: true` reads `viewPadding` instead of `padding`, causing unexpected layout jumps if only `padding` was augmented.
4. **Nested / Local Subtree Issues:** Unlike a top-level `Scaffold`, a generic overlay widget may be nested inside dialogs, split-screen layouts, or custom cards. Mutating `MediaQuery` at an arbitrary position in the tree corrupts window-level metrics for all descendant components.
5. **Rebuild Overhead During Animations:** Using `LayoutBuilder` to push constraint-derived insets through `MediaQuery` forces the entire descendant widget tree to rebuild on every frame when overlay bars animate or resize.

### 2.4 The Proposed Solution: `EdgeInsetsOverlay` (Decoupled & Explicit)

To resolve these issues cleanly, `EdgeInsetsOverlay` decouples overlay measurement from `MediaQuery`:

1. **Edge Docking:** Measures and positions edge overlays (`top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right`) along their corresponding edges using a custom slotted render object.
2. **Explicit Insets Delivery:** Passes the exact computed `EdgeInsets` directly to the `builder` callback (`EdgeInsetsOverlayWidgetBuilder`) or full layout metrics via `EdgeInsetsOverlayMetrics`.
3. **Purity of Ambient State:** Leaves `MediaQuery` completely untouched. The developer has full control over how overlay insets are combined with `MediaQuery.paddingOf(context)` or used independently.
4. **Reliable Layout Invalidation:** Uses custom layout constraints (`_EdgeInsetsOverlayBoxConstraints`) embedding `metrics` to ensure the builder relayouts and rebuilds whenever overlay dimensions change.

## 3. Design & Architecture

### 3.1 `EdgeInsetsOverlay` (Proposed API)

#### API Surface

```dart
/// The slots available for children managed by [EdgeInsetsOverlay].
///
/// See also:
///
///  * [EdgeInsetsOverlay.paintOrder], which configures the order in which child
///    and edge overlay widgets are painted and hit-tested.
enum EdgeInsetsOverlaySlot {
  /// The main content widget built by [EdgeInsetsOverlay.builder] that spans the
  /// full available area beneath overlays.
  child,

  /// The overlay widget docked along the left edge.
  left,

  /// The overlay widget docked along the top edge.
  top,

  /// The overlay widget docked along the right edge.
  right,

  /// The overlay widget docked along the bottom edge.
  bottom,
