issues.body:
### Steps to reproduce

`InAppPurchasePlugin+StoreKit2.swift`'s `finish(id:completion:)` only invokes `completion` when `fetchTransaction` returns a non-nil transaction. When it returns `nil` — or when it throws — `completion` is never called, so the Dart `Future` returned by `InAppPurchase.completePurchase()` never completes.

This was reported and closed in #160148. The fix added `completion(.success(Void()))` **inside** the `if let` branch, so the `nil` and `throw` paths still hang. The code is unchanged on `main` today:

```swift
// packages/in_app_purchase/in_app_purchase_storekit/darwin/in_app_purchase_storekit/
//   Sources/in_app_purchase_storekit/StoreKit2/InAppPurchasePlugin+StoreKit2.swift
func finish(id: Int64, completion: @escaping (Result<Void, Error>) -> Void) {
  Task {
    let transaction = try await fetchTransaction(by: UInt64(id))
    if let transaction = transaction {
      await transaction.finish()
      completion(.success(Void()))
    }
    // transaction == nil  -> completion is never called
    // `try` throws        -> error is swallowed by the Task, completion is never called
  }
}

func fetchTransaction(by id: UInt64) async throws -> Transaction? {
  for await result in Transaction.all {
    switch result {
    case .verified(let transaction):
      if transaction.id == id { return transaction }
    case .unverified:
      continue
    }
  }
  return nil   // reachable
}
```

`fetchTransaction` searches `Transaction.all`. Per Apple's documentation, `Transaction.all` **excludes finished consumables**, so any second `completePurchase()` for a consumable that was already finished lands on the `nil` path and hangs forever.

To reproduce:

1. Use StoreKit 2 (the default since `in_app_purchase_storekit` 0.4.0).
2. Buy a **consumable** product and let it complete normally.
3. Call `InAppPurchase.instance.completePurchase(purchase)` a second time for the same `purchaseID` (this happens naturally when a transaction is redelivered on `purchaseStream`).
4. The returned `Future` never completes and never throws.

### Expected results

`completePurchase()` completes or throws. A transaction that cannot be found is already finished from the caller's point of view, so `completion(.success(Void()))` is the correct answer for the `nil` path.

### Actual results

The `Future` never completes. Without a `.timeout()` the caller is stuck permanently; the transaction is left unfinished, is redelivered on `purchaseStream` on every cold start, and any further purchase attempt for the same product fails with `storekit_duplicate_product_object`.

### Production impact

We ship a consumable-only IAP flow and wrap the call in `.timeout(const Duration(seconds: 3))`, recording the outcome in analytics.

**Every single failure is this hang.** Grouping the failures by exception over the last 14 days yields exactly one group — `TimeoutException after 0:00:03.000000: Future not completed`. No `PlatformException`, no other error type. Successful calls return in ~12 ms, so this is not slowness; the completion handler is simply never invoked.

The share of `completePurchase()` invocations that hang is normally 1–2%, but it is unstable — on one recent day it reached **46%**.

Because a hung call leaves the transaction unfinished, it is redelivered on `purchaseStream` on the next cold start and the app calls `completePurchase()` again, forever. Counting invocations per `purchaseID` over 7 days:

| `completePurchase()` calls for one `purchaseID` | number of transactions |
|---|---|
| **137** | 1 |
| 47 | 4 |
| 38 / 26 / 17 / 7 / 3 / 3 | 1 each |
| exactly 2 | every other transaction |

Only 11 transactions in that window ever exceeded 2 calls, but the ones that do never converge — a single transaction was retried 137 times in 7 days and is still unfinished.

(The baseline of 2 rather than 1 appears to be a separate duplicate-delivery behaviour, cf. #183583.)

The user-visible consequence is that the affected product becomes unbuyable. In one traced case, four unfinished transactions for four different consumable products were redelivered on `purchaseStream` at every cold start, and tapping "buy" on any of those four produced no purchase sheet at all (`storekit_duplicate_product_object`), while a fifth product with no stuck transaction opened normally.

### Suggested fix

```swift
func finish(id: Int64, completion: @escaping (Result<Void, Error>) -> Void) {
  Task {
    do {
      let transaction = try await fetchTransaction(by: UInt64(id))
      if let transaction = transaction {
        await transaction.finish()
      }
      completion(.success(Void()))
    } catch {
      completion(.failure(error))
    }
  }
}
```

Separately, `fetchTransaction` may be better served by `Transaction.unfinished` than `Transaction.all`: `finish()` only ever needs to act on an unfinished transaction, and `Transaction.all` walks the customer's entire purchase history on every call.

### Code sample

<details open><summary>Code sample</summary>

```dart
// Consumable purchase flow. `completePurchase` never completes for a
// transaction that is no longer in `Transaction.all`.
_iap.purchaseStream.listen((List<PurchaseDetails> list) {
  for (final purchase in list) {
    _handlePurchase(purchase);
  }
});

Future<void> _handlePurchase(PurchaseDetails purchase) async {
  if (purchase.status == PurchaseStatus.purchased) {
    await _verifyOnServer(purchase);
  }
  if (purchase.pendingCompletePurchase) {
    // Without the timeout this await never returns.
    await _iap.completePurchase(purchase).timeout(const Duration(seconds: 3));
  }
}
```

</details>

### Logs

<details open><summary>Logs</summary>

```console
TimeoutException after 0:00:03.000000: Future not completed
```

</details>

### Flutter Doctor output

<details open><summary>Doctor output</summary>

```console
[✓] Flutter (Channel stable, 3.41.4, on macOS 26.2 25C56 darwin-x64, locale ko-KR)
    • Flutter version 3.41.4 on channel stable
    • Framework revision ff37bef603, 2026-03-03
    • Engine hash 99578ad0355da00edb26301c874a3c250a5716f5
    • Dart version 3.11.1 • DevTools 2.54.1
[✓] Xcode - develop for iOS and macOS (Xcode 26.2)
[✓] Network resources

Packages:
    • in_app_purchase 3.3.0
    • in_app_purchase_storekit 0.4.11
    • StoreKit 2 (plugin default)
```

</details>

