    dependency: transitive
    description:
      name: in_app_purchase_android
      sha256: f7327b5fd70d8dc1b419fb4cd5c17520175c4059441afd985e8a9c3fa2cefed9
      url: "https://pub.dev"
    source: hosted
    version: "0.5.3"
  in_app_purchase_platform_interface:
    dependency: transitive
    description:
      name: in_app_purchase_platform_interface
      sha256: "0b0076cac8ce4fa7048f01e76af8b123aeb6a7c4e0dea2a5206d6664454f3e36"
      url: "https://pub.dev"
    source: hosted
    version: "1.4.1"
  in_app_purchase_storekit:
    dependency: transitive
    description:
      name: in_app_purchase_storekit
      sha256: bb87b9003fd77da6f23f82c09b1891b28f7bf2520cfa1881a266a9601e675bf0
      url: "https://pub.dev"
    source: hosted
    version: "0.4.13"
```

</details>


### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a subscription group with two auto-renewable subscriptions at different levels:
   - premium_yearly, level 1 (higher)
   - basic_monthly, level 2 (lower)

   Use a StoreKit configuration file in Xcode or App Store Connect with a Sandbox account.
2. Run the code sample below on an iOS device (StoreKit 2 is the default in in_app_purchase_storekit 0.4.13).
3. Tap Buy premium_yearly and complete the purchase. The log shows a purchased event for premium_yearly with a new purchaseID.
4. While premium_yearly is still active, tap Buy basic_monthly and confirm the purchase sheet. The App Store treats this as a downgrade that takes effect at the next renewal.
5. Check the log: the new event is for productID: premium_yearly with the same purchaseID as in step 3. Nothing shows that basic_monthly was bought or is scheduled.
6. Look for any API in in_app_purchase / in_app_purchase_storekit that returns the subscription status or renewal info (RenewalInfo.autoRenewPreference, willAutoRenew) for the group. There isn't one, so the app can't tell the user that the downgrade is scheduled or when it takes effect.

### Expected results

After buying a lower-level subscription while a higher-level subscription in the same group is active (a downgrade), the app should be able to find out that a plan change is scheduled. It needs to know which product renews next and when, so it can tell the user, for example: "Your subscription switches to Basic Monthly on 2026-09-17."

StoreKit 2 provides this through Product.SubscriptionInfo.status(for:) → RenewalInfo.autoRenewPreference / willAutoRenew. in_app_purchase_storekit should make these values available in Dart.
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/storekit/product/subscriptioninfo/renewalinfo

### Actual results

- buyNonConsumable for the lower-level product succeeds.
- purchaseStream emits a PurchaseDetails with status purchased for the currently active higher-level product (productID: premium_yearly), with the same purchaseID as the existing transaction. No transaction for basic_monthly is created until the next renewal.
- in_app_purchase_storekit 0.4.13 has no API to read the subscription status or renewal info (autoRenewPreference, willAutoRenew). The plugin only uses renewalInfo internally for isWinBackOfferEligible.

As a result, the app can't show the user that the downgrade went through or when it takes effect. The UI still looks as if nothing happened.

### Code sample

<details open><summary>Code sample</summary>

Reproduce with a StoreKit configuration file or the Sandbox:
- one subscription group containing `premium_yearly` (level 1) and `basic_monthly` (level 2)
- buy `premium_yearly` first, then tap "Buy basic_monthly"

```dart
import 'dart:async';

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:in_app_purchase/in_app_purchase.dart';

const _kIds = {'premium_yearly', 'basic_monthly'};

void main() => runApp(const MaterialApp(home: DowngradePage()));

class DowngradePage extends StatefulWidget {
  const DowngradePage({super.key});

  @override
  State<DowngradePage> createState() => _DowngradePageState();
}

class _DowngradePageState extends State<DowngradePage> {
  final _iap = InAppPurchase.instance;
  late final StreamSubscription<List<PurchaseDetails>> _sub;
  List<ProductDetails> _products = [];
  final _log = <String>[];

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _sub = _iap.purchaseStream.listen((purchases) async {
      for (final p in purchases) {
        setState(() => _log.add(
            'status: ${p.status}, productID: ${p.productID}, purchaseID: ${p.purchaseID}'));
        if (p.pendingCompletePurchase) {
          await _iap.completePurchase(p);
        }
      }
    });
    _iap.queryProductDetails(_kIds).then((r) {
      setState(() => _products = r.productDetails);
    });
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _sub.cancel();
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Downgrade repro')),
      body: ListView(
        children: [
          for (final product in _products)
            ListTile(
              title: Text('Buy ${product.id}'),
              onTap: () => _iap.buyNonConsumable(
                purchaseParam: PurchaseParam(productDetails: product),
              ),
            ),
          const Divider(),
          // After buying basic_monthly while premium_yearly is active, this
          // shows `productID: premium_yearly` with the old purchaseID.
          // There is no API to find out that basic_monthly is scheduled
          // as the next renewal (RenewalInfo.autoRenewPreference).
          for (final line in _log) ListTile(title: Text(line)),
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

