Steps to reproduce
Take a picture while a video recording is in progress — a combination explicitly supported since camera_android 0.10.8+3 ("Adds support for concurrently capturing images and image streaming/recording").

```

final List<CameraDescription> cameras = await availableCameras();
final CameraController controller = CameraController(
  cameras.first,
  ResolutionPreset.high,
  enableAudio: false,
);
await controller.initialize();

await controller.startVideoRecording();
await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 3));

// Supported since 0.10.8+3.
await controller.takePicture();
await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 3));

final XFile video = await controller.stopVideoRecording();

print(video.path);
// .../cache/CAP7719046239.jpg      <-- a JPEG, not the recording

final Uint8List head = (await File(video.path).readAsBytes()).sublist(0, 4);
print(head);
// [255, 216, 255, 225]             <-- ff d8 ff e1 = JPEG SOI + APP1

```

Expected results
stopVideoRecording() returns the path of the REC*.mp4 created by startVideoRecording().

Actual results
It returns the path of the CAP*.jpg created by the intervening takePicture().

No exception is thrown. MediaRecorder finalizes the real REC*.mp4 correctly — the recording is fine, only the returned path is wrong, and that file is left orphaned in the cache directory with nothing referencing it.

Because the returned XFile is a plausible, non-empty, fully-written file, size- or readability-based validation does not catch it. An app that saves or uploads whatever stopVideoRecording() hands back will silently store a photo in place of the video. We hit this in production: a recording was stored and served as video/mp4 while the bytes were a complete JPEG, and the real video was lost when the OS reclaimed the cache directory.

Root cause

```
// Camera.java uses a single field for both still capture and video recording, and nothing saves or restores it:
private File captureFile;                                       // line 138

// takePicture()      — line 640
captureFile = File.createTempFile("CAP", ".jpg", outputDir);

// prepareRecording() — line 1303
captureFile = File.createTempFile("REC", ".mp4", outputDir);

// stopVideoRecording() — line 922
String path = captureFile.getAbsolutePath();                    // whichever was set last
captureFile = null;
return path;
```
takePicture() also has no recordingVideo guard — its only precondition is cameraCaptureCallback.getCameraState() != CameraState.STATE_PREVIEW, which an in-progress video recording passes — so it neither declines nor warns; it simply overwrites the field.

The only captureFile = null after a picture sits in startVideoRecording()'s catch block, so the JPEG path persists until stopVideoRecording() reads it.

Line numbers above are from 0.10.11+1; the same code exists in 0.10.10 at lines 131 / 620 / 1241 / 868.

Suggested fix
Separate the two paths — e.g. a dedicated videoRecordingFile field set by prepareRecording() and read by stopVideoRecording(), leaving captureFile to still capture. A narrower fix would be to save and restore captureFile around takePicture().

Code sample
See "Steps to reproduce" above.

Logs / evidence
The returned file's header, read straight from the path stopVideoRecording() returned:

magic bytes : ff d8 ff e1       -> JPEG SOI + APP1
trailer     : ff d9             -> complete, valid JPEG
EXIF        : samsung, SM-X205, Software Version v1.1.0
The EXIF capture timestamp matches the moment takePicture() was called, ~8 minutes before stopVideoRecording() was invoked — confirming it is the picture's file and not a truncated recording.

Affected versions
Reproduced on camera_android 0.10.10, and the same code is present in 0.10.11+1 (latest, published 2026-08-28). Nothing in the changelog between those versions touches it.

Note camera_android_camerax is not a workaround for apps that need bitrate/fps control: android_camera_camerax.dart calls proxy.newRecorder(qualitySelector: ...) without targetVideoEncodingBitRate, and has no fps handling at all (see #187149).

Related: #96676 (proposal to take a picture while video recording is in progress — the feature this bug breaks).

Device
Samsung SM-X205 (Galaxy Tab A8), Android.

